	<div class="text-center">
		<h1>
			Summary (1988 Paris - Algiers - Dakar)
		</h1>
		<p>
			<img src="1988_map.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			By 1988, the Mitsubishi Pajero was already established as the top machine in all classes,
			especially in the challenging T1 class, in which the standard version of the King
			of the Desert dominated completely. The tenth Paris - Algiers - Dakar, set at 13,000
			km, was once again to be a car-destroying, faith-destroying challenge,
			leading from the Sahara into the savannahs and bushlands of West Africa.
			The three top drivers hired to bring Mitsubishi to the front were
			Marathon Man Andrew Cowan, Japanese star Kenjiro Shinozuka and Frenchman Pierre
			<img src="1988_01.jpg" alt="" style="float:right" />
			Lartigue. The three were deployed in Sonauto Pajeros in the prototype class,
			Cowan and Lartigue sponsored by Nikon and Shinozuka by Citizen, two
			very important partners in Mitsubishi's competition history. Enormous pressure
			rested on Shinozuka after Cowan and Lartigue retired from the competition
			unusually early. It became the toughest and most professional running there had ever
			been. The battle was fierce, but Shinozuka closed in with great strides on the leader,
			Juha Kankkunen in the Peugeot, until unexpectedly the last long section through
			<img src="1988_02.jpg" alt="" style="float:left" />
			the desert of Mauritania was cancelled and the Japanese driver was robbed of his
			chance of victory. Nevertheless, second place overall in a starting field of 597 after
			such a hard rally was a personal triumph for Shinozuka. Mitsubishi could
			once again celebrate an outstanding performance, as Frenchman Belveze took
			first place in the T1 class.
		</p>
